Kafka is the authoritative history; the PostgreSQL tables and MongoDB collection are query-optimized indexes that can be discarded and rebuilt. A full replay is only possible while Kafka retention preserves the required history, so an event-sourced log cannot use ordinary short-lived retention without another durable archive. The single partition supplies the total order used by this exercise but bounds one projector's parallelism. PostgreSQL commits its two tables transactionally, but that transaction cannot include MongoDB, so a crash may leave the two stores at different offsets. Production projectors record event IDs and per-view checkpoints, make event application idempotent, and resume or replay after failures. Large rebuilds commonly write a versioned shadow projection and switch readers only after it catches up, avoiding a long interval of empty or partially rebuilt views.

The completed `setup.sql` applies every task to the database already seeded by the lab image. The first update changes five duplicated strings. The normalized design then moves company names into a lookup table so a later rename touches exactly one row; all five Acme postings pick up the new name through the FK join.

```sql
-- PostgreSQL
-- Denormalized rename
UPDATE ch2_job_postings_raw
SET    company_name = 'Acme Technologies'
WHERE  company_name = 'Acme Corp';

SELECT id, title, company_name
FROM   ch2_job_postings_raw
WHERE  company_name = 'Acme Technologies';
-- 5 rows: Backend Engineer, Data Analyst, iOS Developer, Product Manager, Marketing Manager

-- Normalize the schema
CREATE TABLE ch2_companies      (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE ch2_cities         (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE ch2_job_categories (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL);

CREATE TABLE ch2_job_postings (
  id          SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  title       TEXT NOT NULL,
  company_id  INT REFERENCES ch2_companies(id),
  city_id     INT REFERENCES ch2_cities(id),
  category_id INT REFERENCES ch2_job_categories(id)
);

INSERT INTO ch2_companies      (name) SELECT DISTINCT company_name FROM ch2_job_postings_raw;
INSERT INTO ch2_cities         (name) SELECT DISTINCT city         FROM ch2_job_postings_raw;
INSERT INTO ch2_job_categories (name) SELECT DISTINCT category     FROM ch2_job_postings_raw;

INSERT INTO ch2_job_postings (title, company_id, city_id, category_id)
SELECT p.title, c.id, ci.id, cat.id
FROM   ch2_job_postings_raw p
JOIN   ch2_companies     c   ON c.name   = p.company_name
JOIN   ch2_cities        ci  ON ci.name  = p.city
JOIN   ch2_job_categories cat ON cat.name = p.category;

UPDATE ch2_companies SET name = 'Acme Corp Inc' WHERE name = 'Acme Technologies';
-- 1 row updated — all 5 postings reflect the new name automatically.

SELECT jp.title, c.name AS company, ci.name AS city, cat.name AS category
FROM   ch2_job_postings jp
JOIN   ch2_companies     c   ON c.id   = jp.company_id
JOIN   ch2_cities        ci  ON ci.id  = jp.city_id
JOIN   ch2_job_categories cat ON cat.id = jp.category_id
WHERE  c.name = 'Acme Corp Inc';
-- 5 rows with company = Acme Corp Inc
```

Tradeoff: a denormalized rename rewrites and locks every matching posting row and generates work and WAL proportional to the number of duplicated values. The single PostgreSQL UPDATE shown here is atomic, but duplicated values can still diverge through separate application writes or later stale writes. Normalization makes the rename one row but reads need JOINs. For write-heavy workloads with frequent name changes, normalization wins; for read-heavy workloads where names rarely change, a denormalized copy can coexist with a normalized source of truth.

`$lookup` performs the equality join in MongoDB and stores matches in an array. `$unwind` turns that array into one joined document per match; preserving an empty result retains the orphan order with missing projected customer fields. The completed `document_lookup.py` is:

```python
from __future__ import annotations

import os

from pymongo import MongoClient


MONGO_URL = os.environ["MONGO_URL"]
MONGO_DB = os.environ["MONGO_DB"]


def joined_orders(db) -> list[dict]:
    pipeline = [
        {"$match": {"status": "paid", "total": {"$gte": 50}}},
        {
            "$lookup": {
                "from": "customers",
                "localField": "customer_id",
                "foreignField": "_id",
                "as": "customer",
            }
        },
        {"$unwind": {"path": "$customer", "preserveNullAndEmptyArrays": True}},
        {
            "$project": {
                "_id": 0,
                "order_id": "$_id",
                "total": 1,
                "customer_name": "$customer.name",
                "tier": "$customer.tier",
            }
        },
        {"$sort": {"order_id": 1}},
    ]
    return list(db.orders.aggregate(pipeline))


def main() -> None:
    with MongoClient(MONGO_URL) as client:
        client.admin.command("ping")
        rows = joined_orders(client[MONGO_DB])

    shape = [
        (row["order_id"], row["total"], row.get("customer_name"), row.get("tier"))
        for row in rows
    ]
    for row in shape:
        print(row)
    assert shape == [
        (1001, 120, "Alice", "gold"),
        (1003, 75, "Alice", "gold"),
        (1004, 50, "Carol", "bronze"),
        (1005, 60, None, None),
    ]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

The native seed check reports 3 customers and 5 orders. With `preserveNullAndEmptyArrays=True`, the script prints `(1001, 120, "Alice", "gold")`, `(1003, 75, "Alice", "gold")`, `(1004, 50, "Carol", "bronze")`, and `(1005, 60, None, None)` in order; pending order `1002` is absent. Temporarily setting the option to `False` drops orphan order `1005` and raises `AssertionError` against the supplied four-row expected shape, so it must be restored to `True`.

Keeping only `customer_id` and joining at read time returns the current customer record without duplicating it, but the read crosses collections and requires the lookup work. Embedding a customer snapshot improves order-read locality and preserves the name printed on a historical invoice, but changing a current name does not rewrite old snapshots automatically. A common design keeps the reference for current customer data while deliberately embedding immutable billing fields whose historical value matters.
